On 2006-08-01, John H. wrote:
> Most of this was brainstormed in a conversation with Pasi....
>
> 1. Safes.  Considering that priests tend to be vulnerable to high-level
> pets, which lets you have your money and spend it too, and also makes
> it maybe easier than it should be to get lots of gold from dead
> shopkeepers.  A safe is a locked box that is typically carried by
> shopkeepers, priests, and would be held by any future monsters who have
> financial dealings with players.
>
> These boxes can be opened and closed by shopkeepers and priests and
> gold deposited and removed basically instantly.  They would not keep
> all their gold in it, more like around half of it.  If the shopkeeper
> or priest is killed, the safe would be dropped along with his pocket
> gold.
>
> Safes would basically always be seen by the player as locked.  They
> would be able to hold both gold and small (as determined by its
> encumberance) items.  Safes could be opened with a wand of opening or a
> spell of knock, but beyond that would be quite difficult to get inside.
>  Other ways to get in would involve a type of random key (see below),
> picking the lock with a very high dexterity (18+), or applying a
> stethoscope.

I like that. Maybe even make safes _heavy_, and part of the shop/shrine
inventory rather than shopkeeper/priest inventory. Add code to make the
owner of the safe protect it while still alive. Money given to the owner
would be instantly (magic, of course) transferred to the safe anyway.

> 2. Metal doors.  In addition to the standard wooden doors, there would
> be added metal doors which cannot be circumvented so easily.  Kicking
> would not work with them unless the kicker has very high (18/** or 25)
> strength.  Wands of opening and knock would work (giving them more use)
> but forcing locks and most other methods of destroying doors would not
> work.  They would be used in some special levels to block off some
> treasure, and could be generated deeper in the main dungeon blocking
> some closets, but typically opening them would not be required to make
> progress in the dungeon, although perhaps they could be used to
> restrict access to sanctum items.  While metal doors cannot be dug or
> phased through, they could be dug around or the walls bordering them
> phased through (level properties allowing).  Some polymorph forms may
> also be able to take care of the doors, especially giants and
> metalvores.

I like this too, because it, like safes, requires the player to be more
resourceful. Definitely make sure that no really vital items are blocked,
and that these doors don't appear too early, when there aren't many
resources to be found yet.

> 3. Random keys.  These two features would make useful again an old
> feature long gone from the game: shaped keys.  In prior versions of
> Nethack, there were doors with locks that required different keys to
> open.  Eventually (to my knowledge) it was decided that collecting all
> those keys, finding the right ones to use on each door, and then using
> them, for each door, was too much hassle to go through for a fairly
> minor part of the game.
>
> This idea involves bringing them back, but not to be used on different
> door locks, but as a random item type.  The skeleton key, that is
> currently the only kind of key in the game, would become one of those
> random types.  Other types would be master keys (for metal doors) and
> safe keys (for safes, natch).  Attempting to use a key on a lock it's
> not made for has a chance of breaking the key.

This I definitely _don't_ like. It would still be just a matter of
collecting all the key types and you're done. Not much difference to the
original system. I would much prefer it when the player is required to
think of other ways to bypass locks than keys. You have already mentioned
several nice methods above.

